import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Evaluation — the fourth dimension","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/explanation/evaluation.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/explanation/evaluation.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/explanation/evaluation.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="evaluation-—-the-fourth-dimension" tabindex="-1">Evaluation — the fourth dimension <a class="header-anchor" href="#evaluation-—-the-fourth-dimension" aria-label="Permalink to “Evaluation — the fourth dimension”">​</a></h1><p>mlopt splits into four independent dimensions; this page is the <strong>evaluation</strong> one — how a program is described, ranked, and measured <em>without letting any of that change what is equivalent</em>.</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>SEMANTICS ──▶ SEARCH ──▶ EVALUATION ──▶ EXECUTION</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>mlopt_core.*</code> modules named below ship in the <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/eqsat" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>eqsat</code></a> substrate repo, pinned here as a git dep; <code>mlopt_torch</code>, <code>mlopt_discovery</code>, <code>scripts/</code>, and <code>bench/</code> are this repo&#39;s.</p><h2 id="the-pieces" tabindex="-1">The pieces <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pieces" aria-label="Permalink to “The pieces”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Static features</strong> (<code>mlopt_core.features</code>) — <code>ProgramFeatures</code> describes a program without running it: FLOPs, bytes read/written, temporary bytes, depth, operations, reuse (arithmetic intensity), parallelism. Torch-free. Features describe a <em>program</em>; a <code>TargetProfile</code> describes a <em>measured target</em>.</li><li><strong>Calibration</strong> (<code>scripts/calibrate_profile.py</code>) — micro-benchmarks the machine it runs on (peak FLOPS, bandwidth, launch overheads, a per-op-class kernel table) and writes a loadable <code>TargetProfile</code> JSON, so <code>delivered_cost_for(profile, x=…)</code> prices extraction by <em>this machine&#39;s</em> numbers, not built-in constants.</li><li><strong>The frontier</strong> (<code>mlopt_core.pareto</code>) — keep the landscape, don&#39;t scalarize early: <code>pareto_frontier</code> keeps non-dominated candidates, <code>best</code> is the scalar view over them.</li><li><strong>Policies</strong> (<code>mlopt_core.policies</code>, <code>mlopt_torch.learned_policy</code>) — a <code>Policy</code> only <em>orders</em> the legal moves, so it can never change what is certified. The learned arm scores <code>(program features ⊕ structural rule vector)</code> — a rule the model has never seen is scored by its shape, not a vocabulary index.</li><li><strong>Performance prediction</strong> (<code>mlopt_core.perf_model</code>) — a roofline <code>max(compute, memory) + launch</code> that <em>ranks</em>; measured to change the extracted program in 0/7 families, so it is a post-hoc ranking tool, not a target chooser.</li><li><strong>Failure taxonomy</strong> (<code>mlopt_core.failures</code>) — <code>OOM</code> / <code>TIMEOUT</code> / <code>KERNEL</code> / <code>NONFINITE</code> / <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> give measurement failures a vocabulary instead of an ambiguous crash.</li></ul><h2 id="screening-—-evaluation-before-search" tabindex="-1">Screening — evaluation before search <a class="header-anchor" href="#screening-—-evaluation-before-search" aria-label="Permalink to “Screening — evaluation before search”">​</a></h2><p>Evaluation also decides where search is worth running at all. The exposure screen scores each corpus workload by the FLOPs-weighted share of ops any law&#39;s left-hand side can name; the distribution is <strong>bimodal</strong> (most workloads expose everything or nothing), and exposure predicts <em>reachability</em>, not <em>payoff</em>. The measured lesson: run discovery where structure is exposed, and let the measured referee — not the search budget — decide what ships.</p><h2 id="the-rule" tabindex="-1">The rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rule" aria-label="Permalink to “The rule”">​</a></h2><p>A profiler <strong>observes</strong>, a policy <strong>orders</strong>, a model <strong>ranks</strong> — none of them decides equivalence. Feasibility (<code>supported_ops</code>, hard) and performance (soft) stay distinct, which is what lets <a href="./three-costs.html">the three costs</a> compose honestly: each level exists because the cheaper one can lie.</p><p>The full account is <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/graphopt/blob/main/docs/evaluation.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>docs/evaluation.md</code></a> upstream.</p>`,11)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};